  "id": "Compound::DB00200 - Gene::25974",
  "verbalization": "Hydroxocobalamin, a form of cobalamin, is implicated in the relationship with Mmachc through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Hydroxocobalamin binds to Mmab, a protein involved in cobalamin transport and metabolism, which in turn is part of the Cobalamin Biosynthetic Process and the Cobalamin (cbl, Vitamin B12) Transport And Metabolism pathways. These pathways ultimately lead to Mmachc, indicating a potential role in the biological processes that regulate cobalamin homeostasis. The binding of Hydroxocobalamin to Mmab may modulate the activity of Mmachc, potentially influencing cobalamin metabolism. The cumulative evidence from the three paths supports a mechanistic link between Hydroxocobalamin and Mmachc, particularly through the involvement of Mmab in cobalamin-related pathways. The observed scores suggest a consistent and plausible relationship, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct effect of Hydroxocobalamin on Mmachc.",
